package Vixen.Shaders.Water

import Vixen.Shaders.Core
import Vixen.Shaders.Geometry

// The water surface: one instanced grid patch, morphed into its parent, sampling the info field and
// displaced by the sea state — docs/plan/35 § D4.
//
// **The terrain's quadtree with a different height source, and that is the whole design.** Every
// patch a frame draws is the *same* grid — 33² vertices for a 32-quad patch — scaled and placed by a
// per-node record, so all the water in a zone is one draw call however many patches it takes. The
// morph is `PatchSelector.MorphIndex`, shared with `Terrain.rvn` by being the same arithmetic, and
// `WaterSurfaceMeshTests` holds this against the C# form because a shader cannot be asked whether two
// adjacent patches agree along their shared edge and a function can.
//
// ⚠ **Water makes a crack worse than a terrain does, not better.** A crack in a terrain shows a
// sliver of skybox for a frame; a crack in a flat specular surface shows a bright line that reads as
// a rendering artefact from four hundred metres. That is why the no-crack test is written before this
// file rather than after the first screenshot.
//
// **What it writes is two planes, not a lit pixel.** § D8's `!Water` pass is what integrates
// absorption and scattering over the depth of water and composites it once; this stage's job is to
// say, per pixel, *where the surface is, how much water is here, which way it faces and whether it is
// foaming*. Splitting them that way is what lets the volume integration be one full-screen pass over
// the pixels that have water rather than a per-fragment loop over the ones that draw it.

/// One patch of water a frame draws.
///
/// The per-instance record the renderer uploads, one per node the selector chose.
///
/// ⚠ **The origin is in world metres and not in texels**, which is the one place this differs from
/// `TerrainNode`. A terrain's patches are addressed in sample coordinates because its heightmap is an
/// atlas of tiles; water's field is a single sliding window, so a world position is both what the
/// wave sum needs and what the field lookup is one subtraction away from — and carrying texels would
/// mean converting back for every wave.
struct WaterNode {
    /// Where the patch's low corner is, on the ground plane, in world metres.
    var origin: float2

    /// How many metres one step of the grid patch spans. `quads * metresPerQuad / gridQuads`.
    var step: float

    /// How far the patch has morphed towards its parent's grid, 0…1.
    var morph: float
}

/// The two planes the surface pass writes, in attachment order.
///
/// ⚠ **Coverage rather than a shading-model id.** § D8 says to classify from the G-buffer's
/// shading-model id "which already exists" — it does not; the deferred path is ⬜ and this engine is
/// Forward+. So the surface writes a one-channel mask, which is exactly what an id comparison would
/// have produced, and when the deferred path lands `Water.rvn`'s binding becomes that comparison with
/// nothing else changing.
struct WaterSurfaceTargets {
    /// Device depth in `r`, coverage in `g`. `Water.rvn` reads both.
    var surface: float4

    /// The surface's world normal in `xyz` and its foam in `a`.
    var normal: float4
}

/// How the field's four channels become the five numbers a surface needs.
///
/// ⚠ **Neither depth nor coverage is a channel, and both are derived here** — § D3's rule, applied
/// twice. Storing either would be a number that can disagree with the two it came from, and the frame
/// it disagrees on is the one where the shoreline is in a different place for the material than for
/// the wave attenuation.
struct WaterInfo {
    /// Where the surface sits at rest, in world units.
    var surfaceHeight: float

    /// How fast the water moves, in metres per second, on the ground plane.
    var flow: float2

    /// Where the ground under it is, in world units.
    var groundHeight: float

    /// How deep the water is — the surface against the ground, never negative.
    static func DepthOf(sample: float4): float => max(sample.r - sample.a, 0f)

    /// How much water is here at all, 0..1.
    ///
    /// The shoreline band, in metres of depth, is what turns a hard edge into a beach. A body's own
    /// `ShoreFalloff` already softened the *field*; this is the last few centimetres, where the
    /// surface meets the bed and a step would read as a cut in the terrain from a long way off.
    static func CoverageOf(sample: float4, shore: float): float {
        return WaterMath.SmoothStep(0f, max(shore, 1e-4f), DepthOf(sample))
    }
}

shader WaterMesh {
    /// How many quads the shared grid patch spans. A compile-time constant so the morph can fold.
    [Permutation] val GridQuads: int = 32

    /// Whether this draw is the skirt to the horizon rather than the window.
    ///
    /// § D4's far mesh: "one more node level past the selector's range, drawn with a permutation that
    /// skips the wave sum and the flow". There is no field out there to sample and nothing to
    /// attenuate against, so the variant is a flat plane at the rest height — which is what the
    /// horizon looks like anyway, and is why it costs one permutation rather than a second shader.
    [Permutation] val FarMesh: bool = false

    /// The sea state.
    ///
    /// A `compose` slot rather than a call, for the reason `IWaterWaves` states: a buffer cannot be a
    /// function parameter, so whoever holds the buffer holds the loop. It is also the seat an FFT
    /// model takes when it lands.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Defaulted to the buffered sum rather than to `StillWater`, and that is what makes the
    /// reflection honest.** Reflection describes one variant, and a binding behind a slot nothing
    /// filled is gone before it is described — so a default of `StillWater` would generate no key for
    /// the wave buffer and the renderer would have to intern one from a string. A document with no sea
    /// state sets `waveCount` to zero, which is the same flat surface for the cost of an empty loop.
    compose val waves: IWaterWaves = BufferedWaterWaves

    var viewProjection: mat4

    /// The patches this draw covers.
    var nodes: Buffer<WaterNode>

    /// The info field: surface height in `r`, flow in `gb`, the ground beneath in `a`.
    var waterInfo: Texture2D
    var infoSampler: Sampler

    /// The window's low corner, on the ground plane, and how many metres it spans.
    var windowOrigin: float2
    var windowExtent: float = 512f

    /// The depth at which waves reach their full height. Shallower than this they are damped.
    var attenuationDepth: float = 2f

    /// The simulation's water time, in seconds. ⚠ Not a frame time — see § D2.
    var waterTime: float = 0f

    /// How wide the band is over which the waves fade out at the window's edge, in metres.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **The seam between this mesh and the far mesh, and the one place the drawn surface is
    /// deliberately not the queried one.** The far mesh has no field under it and so no waves; meeting
    /// them directly puts a step the height of a crest along a straight line at the horizon, in every
    /// frame. `WaterSurfaceMesh.EdgeFade` is the same ramp on the host and states what it costs.
    var edgeFade: float = 32f

    /// Where the open surface sits, for the far mesh, in world units.
    var restHeight: float = 0f

    /// How deep the water has to be before it is fully opaque water, in metres.
    var shoreDepth: float = 0.25f

    /// How shallow the water has to be before it foams, in metres.
    var foamDepth: float = 0.4f

    /// How far above the rest height a crest has to rise before it foams, in metres.
    var foamCrest: float = 0.5f

    stream var positionWS: float3
    stream var normalWS: float3
    stream var coverage: float
    stream var foam: float

    /// The shared patch arithmetic — the morph, the field read, the displacement, the streams.
    ///
    /// `SV_VertexID` rather than a vertex buffer: the grid patch is a regular lattice, so its
    /// positions are two divisions of the index and uploading 33² of them per frame would be sending
    /// the shader something it can count.
    [VertexShader]
    [Semantic("SV_Position")]
    func Vertex(
        [Semantic("SV_VertexID")] vertex: int,
        [Semantic("SV_InstanceID")] instance: int
    ): float4 {
        val node = nodes[instance]
        val side = GridQuads + 1

        val gridX = vertex % side
        val gridZ = vertex / side

        // The morph. An odd index slides back onto its even neighbour, so at morph 1 the patch has
        // exactly half its resolution — which is its parent's, which is why the boundary between the
        // two has nothing to leak through. `PatchSelector.MorphIndex` is the same expression.
        val morphed = float2(
            float(gridX) - float(gridX % 2) * node.morph,
            float(gridZ) - float(gridZ % 2) * node.morph
        )

        val ground = node.origin + morphed * node.step

        if (FarMesh) {
            // No field, no waves, no flow: a plane at the rest height. § D4's far mesh.
            positionWS = float3(ground.x, restHeight, ground.y)
            normalWS = float3(0f, 1f, 0f)
            coverage = 1f
            foam = 0f

            return Transform.ObjectToClip(viewProjection, positionWS)
        }

        val sample = Sample(ground)
        val depth = WaterInfo.DepthOf(sample)

        // ⚠ Two dampings multiplied, and they are different things. The first is § D7's attenuation —
        // a wave whose amplitude does not fall off as the ground rises produces a crest that
        // intersects the beach. The second is the window's own edge, which exists only so the far
        // mesh has something flat to meet.
        val damping = WaterSurface.Attenuation(depth, attenuationDepth) * EdgeDamping(ground)
        val displacement = waves.Displace(ground, waterTime, damping)

        positionWS = float3(
            ground.x + displacement.offset.x,
            sample.r + displacement.offset.y,
            ground.y + displacement.offset.z
        )

        normalWS = displacement.normal
        coverage = WaterInfo.CoverageOf(sample, shoreDepth)
        foam = Foam(depth, displacement.offset.y)

        return Transform.ObjectToClip(viewProjection, positionWS)
    }

    /// The two planes § D8's pass reads.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Discarded where there is no water rather than written as zero.** A patch is selected
    /// conservatively — the coverage predicate rounds outward on purpose, so a tile at the shoreline
    /// carries dry corners — and a dry fragment that wrote a surface depth would put the water's own
    /// depth in front of the beach it is standing on.
    [FragmentShader]
    [Semantic("SV_Target")]
    func Fragment([Semantic("SV_Position")] fragment: float4): WaterSurfaceTargets {
        if (coverage <= 0f) {
            discard
        }

        var targets: WaterSurfaceTargets

        // ⚠ `fragment.z` is the device depth this fragment actually wrote, which is the number
        // `Water.rvn` unprojects. Recomputing it from `positionWS` would be a second answer to a
        // question the rasteriser already settled, and the two disagree by exactly the interpolation
        // error that makes a reconstruction drift across a triangle.
        targets.surface = float4(fragment.z, saturate(coverage), 0f, 0f)
        targets.normal = float4(Math.SafeNormalize(normalWS), saturate(foam))

        return targets
    }

    /// The field at a place on the ground plane.
    ///
    /// ⚠ `SampleLevel` and not `Sample`: a vertex stage has no derivatives, so `Sample` outside a
    /// fragment stage never meant what it looked like — SPIR-V was quietly substituting level zero.
    /// See docs/plan/07, where this was the reason `SampleLevel` landed.
    func Sample(ground: float2): float4 {
        val uv = (ground - windowOrigin) / max(windowExtent, Const.Epsilon)

        return waterInfo.SampleLevel(infoSampler, uv, 0f).rgba
    }

    /// How much of the sea state survives at a place, before the depth attenuation.
    ///
    /// `WaterSurfaceMesh.EdgeDamping` is the same ramp. See `edgeFade`.
    func EdgeDamping(ground: float2): float {
        if (edgeFade <= 0f) {
            return 1f
        }

        val low = ground - windowOrigin
        val high = float2(windowExtent, windowExtent) - low
        val nearest = min(min(low.x, low.y), min(high.x, high.y))

        return WaterMath.SmoothStep(0f, edgeFade, nearest)
    }

    /// Where the surface foams: the shallows, and the tops of the crests.
    ///
    /// Two sources rather than one because they are two different things an observer names — surf on
    /// a beach, and whitecaps in a swell — and a single "foam" term driven by depth alone leaves the
    /// open sea flat however hard the wind blows.
    func Foam(depth: float, lift: float): float {
        val shore = 1f - WaterMath.SmoothStep(0f, max(foamDepth, 1e-4f), depth)
        val crest = WaterMath.SmoothStep(max(foamCrest, 1e-4f), max(foamCrest, 1e-4f) * 2f, lift)

        return max(shore, crest)
    }
}
